<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samudragupta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 335–375 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samudragupta was an emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> who reigned c. 335–375 CE. He transformed the kingdom he inherited from his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandragupta-i">Chandragupta I</a> into a pan-Indian empire through campaigns recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allahabad-pillar-inscription">Allahabad Pillar Inscription</a>. Accounts of the Gupta Empire note his distinction between northern kingdoms that were conquered and absorbed and southern rulers who were defeated and then reinstated as tributaries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samudragupta.jpg" alt="Samudragupta" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SamudraguptaCoin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Samudragupta</b> (c. 335–375 CE) transformed the inheritance into a genuine pan-Indian empire through sustained campaigns recorded in the Allahabad Pillar Inscription composed by his court poet Harishena.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…authority contracted to the Punjab, Gandhara and Kashmir, while Mathura and the Ganges valley passed to local powers such as the Nagas and later to the Gupta Empire; the Allahabad pillar inscription of <b>Samudragupta</b> mentions the homage of a 'Daivaputra-Shahi-Shahanushahi', generally taken to be a late Kushan ruler.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In the mid-fourth century the Allahabad pillar inscription of <b>Samudragupta</b> lists Vishnugopa of Kanchi among the southern kings defeated during the Gupta ruler's southern campaign.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Gupta kings took the title maharajadhiraja, “great king of kings”, and performed the Vedic horse sacrifice; the Allahabad Pillar Inscription praised <b>Samudragupta</b> as a conqueror and a poet.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classical-india">Social Hierarchy of Classical India</a></li><li><blockquote>The Abhira kingdom declined in the 4th century; the Allahabad pillar inscription lists the Abhiras among peoples who submitted to <b>Samudragupta</b> of the Gupta Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/abhira-dynasty">Abhira dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 4th century the Arjunayanas were among the peoples that submitted to <b>Samudragupta</b> of the Gupta Empire, according to his Allahabad pillar inscription, after which they disappear from the record.</blockquote><a href="/state/arjunayanas">Arjunayanas</a></li></ul></section>
